<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mormonism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1820s</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mormonism is the theology and religious tradition of the Latter Day Saint movement of Restorationist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-smith">Joseph Smith</a> in Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in the 1820s and 1830s. Smith said he received a vision in the spring of 1820, and in 1830 he published the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-mormon">Book of Mormon</a>, which he said he had translated from golden plates, and organised the Church of Christ. After Smith was killed by a mob at Nauvoo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, in 1844, the largest group followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigham-young">Brigham Young</a> to what became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah-territory">Utah Territory</a> and practised plural marriage until the 1890 Manifesto. One source estimated that over 400 denominations have sprung from the movement, differing chiefly over polygamy and the Trinity.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Mormonism began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-smith">Joseph Smith</a> in Western New York during the religious excitement of the Second Great Awakening. At fourteen, Smith was troubled by contradictions among the churches available to him and, after reading the Epistle of James, prayed about which to join. He said that in the spring of 1820 God the Father and Jesus Christ appeared to him in what is called the First Vision and told him to join none of the existing churches.</p><p>In 1823, Smith said, the Angel Moroni told him of engraved golden plates buried in a nearby hill. According to Smith he excavated them four years later and translated them from reformed Egyptian into English as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-mormon">Book of Mormon</a>, which recounts the history of a tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a>, led by the prophet Lehi, who migrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> in the 7th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Latter Day Saints hold that God the Father, called Elohim, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, called Jehovah, and the Holy Ghost are three distinct beings, the Father and Jesus having glorified physical bodies. They believe that all people existed first as spirits, that a third of them, led by Satan, rejected God's plan of salvation, and that faithful members may attain godhood in the afterlife.</p><p>They hold that after a Great Apostasy that began not long after the ascension of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, authority was lost and then restored through Smith, when angels including Peter, James, John and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> conferred priesthood authority on him. The scriptural canon is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, held to be generally accurate but incomplete, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-mormon">Book of Mormon</a>, the Doctrine and Covenants and the Pearl of Great Price.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Nearly all Mormons call themselves Christian, though some who define <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> by the doctrines of Catholic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Protestant churches place Mormonism outside it. In the 1830s many American Christians came to see its early doctrines and practices as subversive and heretical, and the discord led to sometimes deadly conflicts that declined in the twentieth century.</p><p>The movement split early, as far back as 1831, and most significantly after Smith's death. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-jesus-christ-of-latter-day-saints">Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints</a> has existed continuously since the succession crisis of 1844 and is by far the largest. It banned polygamy in 1890, under pressure because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> would not be admitted as a state otherwise, and Mormon fundamentalists, who hold that plural marriage is required for exaltation, broke away.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mormonism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
